---
# Slice 2 of the ingress default-deny. NOTE: this role's OTHER policy file, networkpolicy.yaml.j2,
# fences flaresolverr and is unrelated to this one. Both are applied.
#
# This rule is PORT-SCOPED, and for the callers below that scoping is the whole grant: the baseline
# admits none of monitor-bridge, sonarr, radarr or flaresolverr-netpol-probe, so they reach prowlarr
# only through this rule and only on this one port.
#
# TRAEFIK IS LISTED HERE DELIBERATELY — do not remove it as redundant — but be accurate about why.
# The baseline admits traefik to every baseline-labelled pod on ANY port, and policies are
# additive, so while prowlarr carries that label the union is the same with or without this entry.
# It was load-bearing for the deploy that INTRODUCED the label: the old pod carried `app: prowlarr`
# without `netpol-baseline: enforced`, so this policy was the only one selecting it and the UI
# would have 502'd for the length of that rollout (the "rollout hazard" section of the slice-2
# plan). That window is closed — every pod created from here on carries both labels. It stays
# because this policy then states its own complete caller set rather than depending on the baseline
# continuing to admit traefik on all ports: if that baseline rule is ever port-scoped, or this pod
# template loses the label, this line is what keeps the UI up.
#
# sonarr and radarr reach prowlarr by bare name from their own application databases — the indexer
# definitions live on their PVCs, not in this repo. Recorded in
# roles/k8s/sonarr/tasks/main.yml's header.
#
# flaresolverr-netpol-probe is the CONTROL leg of this role's OWN isolation probe
# (netpol-probe-job.yaml.j2): before it asserts flaresolverr is unreachable from outside
# prowlarr, it first proves its own pod networking works at all by retrying
# `nc -z prowlarr {{ container_item.port }}` for up to 150s, riding out prowlarr's restart. Fence
# that leg out and the control can never pass, so the Job fails and aborts every prowlarr deploy
# — a negative test with no working control proves nothing. Same shape as
# roles/k8s/registry/templates/networkpolicy.yaml.j2 admitting its own
# registry-selftest-push/registry-selftest-pull Jobs: a deploy-time test pod that must reach the
# workload it is fencing. Do not "clean this up" as an unused caller.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: prowlarr
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: prowlarr
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: traefik
        - podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values:
                  - monitor-bridge
                  - sonarr
                  - radarr
                  - flaresolverr-netpol-probe  # control leg of this role's own isolation probe
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ container_item.port }}
